<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eucharist</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lord's Supper, Lord’s Supper, Holy Communion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eucharist, also called Holy Communion or the Lord's Supper, is the Christian rite of bread and wine commemorating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Its meaning divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>: Catholics taught transubstantiation, Luther a real bodily presence, and Zwingli a symbolic memorial, a disagreement left unresolved at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> of 1529. It remains the central act of worship in most Christian churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucharist.jpg" alt="Eucharist" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nheyob, cropped by Tahc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Michael_the_Archangel,_Findlay,_OH_-_bread_and_wine_crop_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Christians believe that the rite was instituted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a>, the night before his crucifixion, when he gave his disciples bread and wine. Passages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> state that he told them to do this in memory of him. According to the synoptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> the meal was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> meal. The First Epistle to the Corinthians gives the earliest account and shows how early Christians observed what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> called the Lord's Supper.</p><p>The Didache, which most scholars date to the late 1st century, gives instructions for baptism and the Eucharist, and Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> called the Eucharist the flesh of the Saviour Jesus Christ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name comes from the Greek eucharistia, thanksgiving. The term Lord's Supper came into popular use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, and Communion also began with groups that originated in the Reformation. Most churches treat the rite as a sacrament, others, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a>, as an ordinance. Christians generally recognise a special presence of Christ in the rite but differ on how and where.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> teaches that by the consecration the substances of bread and wine become those of the body and blood of Christ, while their appearances remain. The Eastern and Oriental Orthodox churches agree that an objective change occurs but leave the manner a mystery. Lutherans hold that the body and blood are really present in, with and under the bread and wine, Reformed Christians a real spiritual presence, and the Plymouth Brethren a memorial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the Eastern churches the service is called the Divine Liturgy, in two parts: the Liturgy of the Catechumens, ending in a Gospel reading, and the Liturgy of the Faithful, in which the Eucharist is offered and received. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> churches observe the Holy Qurbana, whose main East Syriac anaphora is that of Addai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and the Liturgy of Saint James; both go back at least to the third century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> the sacrifice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> made once on the cross is believed to be made present at every Mass. The belief that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> desecrated the consecrated host fed medieval anti-Jewish violence, and was related to the blood libel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eucharist” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zwingli’s symbolic understanding of the <b>Eucharist</b>, which he defended against Martin Luther at the Marburg Colloquy of 1529, became a defining feature of Reformed Protestantism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>All the major churches practise baptism, by which a person enters the church, and the <b>Eucharist</b>, also called Holy Communion or the Mass, the shared meal of bread and wine in memory of Jesus’ last supper; Catholic and Orthodox Christians recognise seven sacraments, most Protestant churches two.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian apologists noted similarities and called them devilish imitations: Justin Martyr compared its bread and cup with the <b>Eucharist</b>, and Tertullian likened its ritual bath and mark on the forehead to baptism.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>He reduced the seven sacraments of the medieval Church to those he found instituted by Christ in the Gospels, chiefly baptism and the <b>Lord’s Supper</b>, and called on the German nobility to reform the Church if the clergy would not.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>A hymn of praise for the sacrament of the <b>Eucharist</b>, expressing the belief that in the Mass the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox include it in the prayers before receiving the <b>Eucharist</b>; the Catholic Church assigns it to the Liturgy of the Hours and sings it as a responsorial at Masses for the dead; the Coptic Agpeya prays it at Terce; and the Church of England's Book of Common…</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li></ul></section>
